    stem: "The sketch shows a dwelling house which was built over 40 years ago. The external wall is a 300 mm concrete block wall with a 100 mm cavity. There is no insulation in the cavity. It is proposed to insulate the external wall by: filling the cavity with insulation and adding an external system of insulation to the wall.",
    questionText: "(a) Using notes and freehand sketches, show the steps to be followed in applying each system of insulation to the wall. (b) Discuss two advantages of insulating the external walls of the house.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed"}, totalMarks: 50,
    rows: [
    { id: "a-a-cavity-fill-notes", kind: "criterion", verbatim: "Filling the cavity with insulation – Notes", marks: 12, contextNote: "Three valid steps, 4 marks each. Typical procedure (carried out by specialist firms): 22 mm diameter holes are drilled through the external leaf, usually spaced about 800 mm horizontally and 1350 mm vertically; special care is taken at damp proof courses and at heads of windows and doors, with extra holes drilled around openings so the cavity fills properly; polystyrene beads are pumped into the cavity while a light coating of glue is applied, setting into a solid structure; when pumping is complete the holes are filled. Other valid steps are acceptable.", permittedMarks: [0,4,8,12] },
    { id: "a-a-cavity-fill-sketches", kind: "criterion", verbatim: "Filling the cavity with insulation – Sketches", marks: 8, contextNote: "Quality of sketches for the cavity-fill steps: excellent 8, good 6, fair 4. The scheme sketches show the drill entering the outer leaf and the hole spacing on the wall.", permittedMarks: [0,4,6,8] },
    { id: "a-a-external-insulation-notes", kind: "criterion", verbatim: "Adding an external system of insulation to the wall – Notes", marks: 12, contextNote: "Three valid steps, 4 marks each. Typical procedure: the first row of insulation boards is fixed in place resting on a stainless steel track; boards are fixed in position with a special adhesive, and all rows are fixed and the adhesive allowed to set; mechanical fixings (typically about seven per square metre, longer for thicker boards) are installed by drilling through the insulation into the wall and then hammered into place. Typical insulation materials: polystyrene, mineral fibre board, wood fibre board. Other valid steps are acceptable.", permittedMarks: [0,4,8,12] },
    { id: "a-a-external-insulation-sketches", kind: "criterion", verbatim: "Adding an external system of insulation to the wall – Sketches", marks: 8, contextNote: "Quality of sketches for the external-insulation steps: excellent 8, good 6, fair 4. The scheme sketch shows the wall, adhesive, insulation board and mechanical fixing.", permittedMarks: [0,4,6,8] },
    { id: "b-advantages", kind: "criterion", verbatim: "Two advantages of insulating the external walls of the house", marks: 10, contextNote: "Two distinct advantages, 5 marks each, discussed rather than just named. Such as: reduced heat loss and a warmer house; improved thermal comfort with no extremes of heat or cold; lower energy bills; less fossil fuel burned to heat the house, which is better for the environment; improved U-value and Building Energy Rating (BER); reduced condensation and mould; the wall retains heat and acts as a heat store. Do not credit the same advantage twice.", permittedMarks: [0,5,10] },
    ],
